╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ shorter_lookbacks_zscore_normalization                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ The primary issue is that val_slope collapses to 0.006 (8.2% of train_slope), indicating  │
│                          │ features that work in training don't generalize to the validation period. This suggests   │
│                          │ regime-specific patterns.                                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ My strategy addresses this through three coordinated changes:                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Shorter lookback windows**: Reduce long lookback periods (60, 200) to shorter ones   │
│                          │ (5-20 bars). Shorter windows adapt faster to regime changes and decay less across time    │
│                          │ periods.                                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Rolling z-score normalization**: Instead of raw values, compute z-scores within      │
│                          │ rolling windows. This makes features regime-invariant - a "high RSI" means the same thing │
│                          │ regardless of whether we're in a bull or bear market.                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. **Rate-of-change features**: Add features that measure how market character is         │
│                          │ changing (volatility trend, momentum acceleration) rather than absolute levels. These     │
│                          │ tend to be more stable across regimes.                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 4. **Reduce model complexity**: Lower n_estimators from 400 to 200 and increase           │
│                          │ min_child_weight from 1 to 5. This forces the model to learn only robust patterns with    │
│                          │ high sample support, preventing overfitting to training-period quirks.                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 5. **Feature interaction**: Add a simple momentum × volatility_regime multiplicative term │
│                          │ that often generalizes better than either feature alone.                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The key insight: instead of measuring "price is X% above 60-bar MA" (regime-dependent),   │
│                          │ we measure "price is Z standard deviations above its recent average"                      │
│                          │ (regime-independent).                                                                     │
